1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,250
Have you ever wondered what it
will feel like when you die?

2
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:06,477
Will there be darkness? Silence?

3
00:00:07,357 --> 00:00:11,027
Will you somehow remain conscious,
floating in nothingness? Will you wait?

4
00:00:11,839 --> 00:00:13,919
And if you believe in
an afterlife, perhaps

5
00:00:13,999 --> 00:00:16,169
you imagine waking up somewhere else.

6
00:00:17,548 --> 00:00:20,328
Every one of those answers
sounds like a different thing.

7
00:00:20,408 --> 00:00:23,798
But they all have the same
thing hidden inside them.

8
00:00:24,408 --> 00:00:26,358
Let's take them one at a time.

9
00:00:27,077 --> 00:00:29,527
Darkness. That's usually the first answer.

10
00:00:29,637 --> 00:00:32,047
But darkness is something you see.

11
00:00:32,829 --> 00:00:35,499
Shut your eyes right now
and you still get darkness.

12
00:00:36,609 --> 00:00:40,959
A dim, shifting grey, delivered by a
visual system that is still running.

13
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,170
Silence, then. But silence
is something you hear.

14
00:00:45,910 --> 00:00:49,920
An empty room? An empty room
still has someone standing in it.

15
00:00:50,010 --> 00:00:52,130
And an endless wait?

16
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:54,660
Waiting requires someone who is waiting.

17
00:00:55,591 --> 00:01:00,221
There it is. Every one of those answers
quietly puts someone into the picture.

18
00:01:01,022 --> 00:01:02,612
Someone who sees the dark.

19
00:01:03,042 --> 00:01:06,432
Someone who hears the
silence. Someone who waits.

20
00:01:07,309 --> 00:01:11,419
So before we ask what death feels like,
we have to ask a much harder question.

21
00:01:12,109 --> 00:01:14,059
What happens to that someone?

22
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:19,309
Here is the answer most
people reach for next.

23
00:01:20,159 --> 00:01:21,969
"After I die, I'll sleep forever."

24
00:01:22,897 --> 00:01:25,387
But eternal sleep would
still imply a sleeper.

25
00:01:26,037 --> 00:01:29,907
And sleep has a feature that
death doesn't. You wake up.

26
00:01:30,634 --> 00:01:32,544
Imagine falling asleep tonight.

27
00:01:33,294 --> 00:01:35,774
Then imagine that,
somehow, eight hours simply

28
00:01:35,854 --> 00:01:38,184
disappear from your subjective experience.

29
00:01:39,095 --> 00:01:41,405
You don't experience
eight hours of nothing.

30
00:01:42,035 --> 00:01:44,285
You experience one moment before sleep...

31
00:01:44,975 --> 00:01:46,985
and then another moment when you wake up.

32
00:01:47,671 --> 00:01:51,281
That gap isn't an experience.
It's a missing one.

33
00:01:53,441 --> 00:01:57,931
All right. Try it directly. Try to
imagine nothing. Really nothing.

34
00:01:58,701 --> 00:02:01,211
Most of us immediately
picture a black screen.

35
00:02:01,641 --> 00:02:05,751
But that's not nothing. There
is a screen. There is darkness.

36
00:02:06,695 --> 00:02:09,725
And, most importantly,
there is you, looking at it.

37
00:02:10,396 --> 00:02:14,626
Try again. Imagine an empty
universe. Nothing in it at all.

38
00:02:14,996 --> 00:02:16,546
But who is observing it?

39
00:02:17,216 --> 00:02:20,806
Our brains are simply not very good
at simulating their own absence.

40
00:02:21,116 --> 00:02:23,526
And there is a good reason for that.

41
00:02:25,836 --> 00:02:29,826
Your conscious experience normally
has a very strong sense of continuity.

42
00:02:30,856 --> 00:02:33,766
There was a moment ago. There is now.

43
00:02:34,316 --> 00:02:36,466
And there will be a moment after this one.

44
00:02:37,181 --> 00:02:38,701
Memory gives you a past.

45
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:40,991
Perception gives you the present.

46
00:02:41,161 --> 00:02:43,351
Prediction gives you a future.

47
00:02:43,781 --> 00:02:47,691
And that is what gives you the feeling
of being one continuous person.

48
00:02:48,419 --> 00:02:50,609
Neuroscientists have a phrase for it.

49
00:02:51,179 --> 00:02:53,249
Your brain is a prediction machine.

50
00:02:53,859 --> 00:02:56,469
It is always running a
model of what comes next.

51
00:02:57,201 --> 00:03:00,951
So when you try to imagine your own
death, it does what it always does.

52
00:03:01,861 --> 00:03:03,061
It builds a sequence.

53
00:03:03,849 --> 00:03:05,089
Me. Then death. Then time.

54
00:03:05,169 --> 00:03:07,839
Then more time. Then more time.

55
00:03:08,491 --> 00:03:09,741
But there is a problem.

56
00:03:10,291 --> 00:03:14,781
After the end of consciousness, there is
nobody there to experience that sequence.

57
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:18,940
Your brain keeps adding time
because that's what brains do.

58
00:03:19,710 --> 00:03:23,940
It is trying to simulate a future in which
the simulator itself no longer exists.

59
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,540
There is one situation
that gets close to this,

60
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:33,970
and millions of people go through
it every year. General anaesthesia.

61
00:03:35,403 --> 00:03:37,333
The brain isn't simply switched off.

62
00:03:38,203 --> 00:03:40,363
Parts of it stay active, still processing

63
00:03:40,443 --> 00:03:43,613
information, while conscious
experience is gone.

64
00:03:44,397 --> 00:03:46,407
You can be unconscious for six hours.

65
00:03:46,917 --> 00:03:47,977
Then you open your eyes.

66
00:03:48,057 --> 00:03:49,487
And subjectively?

67
00:03:50,191 --> 00:03:53,061
There isn't a six-hour-long
experience of darkness.

68
00:03:53,531 --> 00:03:56,301
There is no memory of an empty black room.

69
00:03:56,891 --> 00:03:58,301
It feels more like this.

70
00:03:59,038 --> 00:04:00,408
Before... and after.

71
00:04:02,838 --> 00:04:04,628
And somebody actually counted.

72
00:04:05,538 --> 00:04:09,208
Britain gives around three million
general anaesthetics a year.

73
00:04:09,912 --> 00:04:13,282
About one patient in twenty
thousand reports being aware.

74
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,090
Of course, anaesthesia isn't death.

75
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,570
It is temporary and controlled.

76
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:22,190
And most importantly,
anaesthesia has an awakening.

77
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:24,430
Death doesn't.

78
00:04:25,532 --> 00:04:28,882
But it gives us an intuition for
something very difficult to imagine.

79
00:04:29,852 --> 00:04:31,682
Subjective time can disappear.

80
00:04:34,622 --> 00:04:37,812
So let's go back to the simplest
possible version of death.

81
00:04:38,702 --> 00:04:40,312
Your brain stops functioning.

82
00:04:41,141 --> 00:04:45,371
Cut the blood supply, and consciousness
can be lost within about ten seconds.

83
00:04:46,251 --> 00:04:49,941
The processes that create
perception, memory and thought stop.

84
00:04:50,751 --> 00:04:54,001
The integration that produces
conscious experience is gone.

85
00:04:54,797 --> 00:04:57,507
And that is what every one
of those answers missed.

86
00:04:58,391 --> 00:05:00,521
Death is not a state you experience.

87
00:05:01,291 --> 00:05:03,081
It is the absence of experience.

88
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:09,051
Here's a slightly morbid
way of putting it.

89
00:05:09,801 --> 00:05:12,291
Imagine your brain as a gigantic computer.

90
00:05:13,291 --> 00:05:15,981
Processing units. Memory.

91
00:05:16,231 --> 00:05:19,561
And a huge network letting
the parts talk to each other.

92
00:05:20,303 --> 00:05:24,513
The conscious experience isn't a file
sitting somewhere on the hard drive.

93
00:05:25,303 --> 00:05:26,653
It's the running system.

94
00:05:27,463 --> 00:05:29,593
With death, the hardware itself fails.

95
00:05:30,483 --> 00:05:32,013
The connections are destroyed.

96
00:05:32,763 --> 00:05:34,113
The processes stop.

97
00:05:34,943 --> 00:05:38,493
And eventually there isn't even a
computer left to display a black screen.

98
00:05:39,341 --> 00:05:42,131
Death isn't a computer showing
a black screen forever.

99
00:05:42,841 --> 00:05:44,491
It's the computer being gone.

100
00:05:47,031 --> 00:05:49,251
And we have no reliable
evidence for a consciousness

101
00:05:49,331 --> 00:05:52,081
that continues independently of the brain.

102
00:05:52,811 --> 00:05:54,181
Not yet, at least.

103
00:05:56,891 --> 00:05:59,041
Which raises a much better question.

104
00:05:59,911 --> 00:06:03,361
If there is nothing there to feel,
why does it frighten us so much?

105
00:06:04,157 --> 00:06:07,587
Part of the answer is hiding in
our own language. We say this.

106
00:06:08,317 --> 00:06:09,517
"I will be dead forever."

107
00:06:10,399 --> 00:06:12,429
But notice what the sentence does.

108
00:06:13,219 --> 00:06:16,219
It puts you into an enormous
timeline, as a person who remains

109
00:06:16,299 --> 00:06:20,069
there, experiencing the passage
of time while being dead.

110
00:06:20,822 --> 00:06:24,252
The universe can continue for
billions or trillions of years.

111
00:06:24,782 --> 00:06:28,532
None of it reaches someone whose
consciousness no longer exists.

112
00:06:29,453 --> 00:06:31,623
There is no experience of ten years.

113
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:34,783
There is no experience
of ten billion years.

114
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:37,223
There is simply no further experience.

115
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:42,023
And here is the strangest part.

116
00:06:42,693 --> 00:06:44,163
You have already been through this.

117
00:06:44,910 --> 00:06:48,620
Think about the time before you
were born. You don't remember it.

118
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:53,100
Not because your memory is
damaged. Not because you forgot.

119
00:06:53,972 --> 00:06:55,342
You simply weren't there.

120
00:06:58,112 --> 00:07:00,902
You didn't spend billions
of years waiting to be born.

121
00:07:01,452 --> 00:07:05,722
You didn't experience darkness. You
weren't bored. You weren't lonely.

122
00:07:06,596 --> 00:07:09,866
You weren't thinking this.
"When is this going to end?"

123
00:07:10,416 --> 00:07:12,786
There was no you to experience any of it.

124
00:07:13,459 --> 00:07:15,269
And that argument is not new.

125
00:07:16,079 --> 00:07:19,329
A Roman poet wrote it down
about two thousand years ago.

126
00:07:20,042 --> 00:07:23,202
Lucretius asked his readers to
look back at the eternity before

127
00:07:23,282 --> 00:07:26,852
their birth, and to notice
that it had cost them nothing.

128
00:07:29,182 --> 00:07:33,392
So why does the eternity on the other
side feel so completely different?

129
00:07:34,227 --> 00:07:37,047
Because the past is finished,
and the future is the

130
00:07:37,127 --> 00:07:39,777
thing our brain is
constantly trying to control.

131
00:07:40,638 --> 00:07:44,548
So losing it feels like losing
something that already belongs to us.

132
00:07:45,318 --> 00:07:50,148
But it doesn't. There is no future
experience waiting for you after death.

133
00:07:52,778 --> 00:07:56,888
There is one more distinction, and
it may be the most useful one here.

134
00:07:57,610 --> 00:08:00,040
When people say this.
"I'm afraid of death."

135
00:08:00,510 --> 00:08:02,040
They may actually mean this.

136
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:03,860
"I'm afraid of dying."

137
00:08:04,707 --> 00:08:06,277
Those are not the same thing.

138
00:08:08,717 --> 00:08:14,177
Dying can involve pain, fear, confusion,
loss of control, separation from people

139
00:08:14,257 --> 00:08:18,787
you love, or simply the knowledge that
you're about to lose everything familiar.

140
00:08:19,651 --> 00:08:21,591
Those are real experiences.

141
00:08:21,671 --> 00:08:25,361
And medicine can sometimes do
a great deal to reduce them.

142
00:08:26,042 --> 00:08:27,452
But being dead is different.

143
00:08:28,522 --> 00:08:32,312
Once consciousness has ended, there
is no longer anyone experiencing pain.

144
00:08:32,862 --> 00:08:37,312
There is no fear. No boredom.
No loneliness. No waiting.

145
00:08:40,382 --> 00:08:44,032
A conscious brain can even imagine
"Nothing will happen to me."

146
00:08:44,723 --> 00:08:48,253
But it struggles with "There will
be no me to whom anything happens."

147
00:08:49,171 --> 00:08:54,721
So the brain quietly puts itself back into
the picture. An observer. A black room.

148
00:08:55,371 --> 00:08:58,301
A silent universe. An
endless passage of time.

149
00:08:59,042 --> 00:09:01,462
And we end up with
exactly what death isn't.

150
00:09:01,542 --> 00:09:03,152
An experience.

151
00:09:05,902 --> 00:09:08,392
There is something almost
elegant about the symmetry.

152
00:09:09,182 --> 00:09:12,592
Before your existence began,
there was no subjective time.

153
00:09:13,502 --> 00:09:16,652
Then suddenly, there was a first
moment you could experience.

154
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:19,820
And eventually, there will be a last one.

155
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:22,540
After that, the universe continues.

156
00:09:23,330 --> 00:09:25,000
But your timeline doesn't.

157
00:09:25,290 --> 00:09:27,500
There is simply no further point on it.

158
00:09:29,810 --> 00:09:31,940
Which quietly changes the question.

159
00:09:32,770 --> 00:09:35,740
Instead of asking "What will
I experience after death?"

160
00:09:36,607 --> 00:09:40,197
the more useful question might
be "What am I experiencing now?"

161
00:09:42,867 --> 00:09:43,947
Because this part is real.

162
00:09:44,027 --> 00:09:46,077
This moment exists.

163
00:09:46,407 --> 00:09:47,957
The people around you exist.

164
00:09:48,547 --> 00:09:50,357
The things you enjoy exist.

165
00:09:51,007 --> 00:09:54,877
The things you still want to see,
learn, build and experience exist.

166
00:09:55,769 --> 00:09:59,789
There may be no second run.
No backup. No save game.

167
00:09:59,869 --> 00:10:01,699
No loading screen after the end.

168
00:10:02,459 --> 00:10:04,109
And that can sound frightening.

169
00:10:04,999 --> 00:10:06,419
But it can also make the one thing we

170
00:10:06,499 --> 00:10:09,049
actually have, this
life, matter enormously.

171
00:10:12,049 --> 00:10:15,819
Perhaps the strangest thing about
death is not that the lights go out.

172
00:10:16,569 --> 00:10:18,939
It is that there is nobody left to notice.

173
00:10:19,827 --> 00:10:23,457
We are trying to imagine the absence
of the person doing the imagining.

174
00:10:24,247 --> 00:10:27,257
And that may simply be something
consciousness cannot do.
